<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/foundational/how-machines-learn">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    For most of computing's history, a program was answers written by a human. Spam filters ended that:
    nobody can write the rule for what spam looks like — it is "whatever spammers decide this month".
    The fix was a different way of building software. Instead of writing rules, you write a
    <strong>learning loop</strong>: the machine makes a guess, a <em>loss function</em> measures how
    wrong the guess was, calculus supplies the direction of "downhill" (the <strong>gradient</strong>),
    and the machine's adjustable parameters take a small step that way. Repeat a few million times and
    behaviour emerges that no one programmed. That loop — guess, measure, step — is
    <strong>gradient descent</strong>, and in one form or another it trains everything from spam filters
    to ChatGPT-class models. This is the deep sense in which machines "learn": not acquiring
    understanding, but systematically reducing a mistake score against data.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the whole of machine learning in one picture — red dots are training steps rolling downhill to the best answer" widget="plotter" config={descendCfg} />

  <p>
    Each red dot is one training step. The model here has a single parameter <code>w</code>; the purple
    curve is the loss — how wrong the model is — for every possible setting of that parameter. Start at
    w = 0 (badly wrong, loss = 10), measure the slope (steeply negative, so step right), and watch the
    steps shrink as the ground flattens. Near the bottom the gradient is almost zero, so the model stops
    moving: <em>converged</em>, at w = 3, the best answer the data supports. Real networks do this in
    millions or billions of dimensions, where "downhill" can't be drawn, only computed — but the
    geometry is identical, and so is the path: big strides early, tiptoeing at the end, each step size
    set by the <strong>learning rate</strong> times the local slope.
  </p>

  <h2>Learning from examples: the supervised recipe</h2>
  <p>
    The dominant form of machine learning is <strong>supervised learning</strong>: you have examples
    with the right answers attached — emails labelled spam/not-spam, X-rays labelled by radiologists,
    house prices with selling prices recorded. The task is to infer the mapping from inputs to labels,
    and it splits into two families. <strong>Classification</strong> predicts a category (spam or not;
    cat, dog or hamster), while <strong>regression</strong> predicts a number (tomorrow's temperature,
    a house's price). <strong>Unsupervised learning</strong>, by contrast, gets no labels at all — it
    finds structure: which customers cluster together, which words co-occur. <strong>Reinforcement
    learning</strong> is the third paradigm: an agent acts, receives rewards, and learns which actions
    pay off — how systems learned to play Go and control robots. The prototype of supervised learning
    is the <strong>decision tree</strong>: a flowchart of questions learned from data ("is the
    email from an unknown sender? is it asking for a password?") where each internal node tests one
    attribute and each leaf is a prediction. Trees are beloved for one reason: you can read them.
    An algorithm like ID3 chooses each split by <strong>information gain</strong> — the question that
    most reduces uncertainty about the answer goes first — and a tree that perfectly classifies its
    training data can be grown in seconds, which is both the method's power and, as we will see, its
    trap.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the machine does not "figure out the rules" in any human sense.
  It adjusts numbers to reduce a score. A spam filter has no concept of deception; it has weights that
  happened to distinguish the labelled examples. When the world shifts (new spam tactics, a hospital
  changing scanners), the model does not notice — it must be retrained, and the shift has a name,
  <strong>concept drift</strong>.</p>

  <h2>Generalisation: the only score that matters</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the field's central paradox. You train on the data you have, but you are graded on data you
    don't have. A model judged on its <strong>training data</strong> can cheat by memorising — the
    machine-learning name for this is <strong>overfitting</strong>: the model has learned the noise, not
    the pattern. The spammed-up example: a decision tree grown to perfection on last year's emails may
    have leaves like "sender contains the letter q" — a rule that fit the sample and will misfire on
    tomorrow's inbox. The defence is procedural and simple: split the data. Train on one portion,
    evaluate on a <strong>validation set</strong> the model never saw, and tune on that; report final
    performance on a third <strong>test set</strong> touched exactly once. When data is scarce,
    <strong>k-fold cross-validation</strong> rotates the split k times and averages. The tell-tale
    signature of overfitting is a widening gap: training error keeps falling while validation error
    starts to rise — the moment to stop or to simplify. Regularisation (penalising model complexity in
    the loss itself) and pruning (cutting a tree's weakest branches) are the standard curbs, and they
    work because they encode a philosophical bet: <em>simpler explanations that fit the data are more
    likely to extend to new data</em> — Ockham's razor wearing a loss function. Two practical
    corollaries finish the hygiene: <strong>data leakage</strong> — information from the future or from
    the test set (a field the model shouldn't legally see, a scaler fitted on all the data) inflates
    results until the model meets reality, and is the most common way honest-looking evaluations lie —
    and <strong>class imbalance</strong>: a fraud model that labels everything "legitimate" is 99.9%
    accurate and completely useless, which is why imbalanced problems are scored with precision,
    recall and the confusion matrix rather than raw accuracy.
  </p>

  <h2>Neural networks and deep learning</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>neural network</strong> is layers of simple units, each computing a weighted sum of its
    inputs passed through a soft nonlinearity — loosely inspired by neurons, mostly just useful
    mathematics. One layer of units can only draw simple boundaries; two or more
    (<strong>hidden layers</strong>) can approximate almost any function — that is the
    <strong>universal approximation theorem</strong>, and it explains both the ambition and the
    difficulty. The difficulty was training: with a million weights, which way is downhill? The answer
    — <strong>backpropagation</strong>, popularised by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams in 1986 — is
    gradient descent organised by the chain rule: compute the error at the output, pass it backwards
    layer by layer, each weight learning its own share of the blame. That single algorithm made
    multilayer networks trainable, and modern deep learning is essentially backprop plus scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    What makes learning <em>deep</em> is depth used for <strong>representation learning</strong>: early
    layers learn simple features (edges in an image, syllables in text), middle layers combine them
    (eyes, wheels; phrases), late layers combine those (faces, vehicles; sentences) — a hierarchy built
    from the data rather than designed by hand. <strong>Convolutional networks</strong> (LeCun, late
    1980s) hard-wire one insight — a feature useful in one corner of an image is useful everywhere — by
    sliding small filters across the whole picture, which slashed the number of parameters and made
    vision practical. The inflection point came from three compounding trends: data (ImageNet's
    ~1.2 million labelled images), compute (GPUs, built for games, turned out to be gradient machines),
    and algorithmic tricks. In 2012 a deep convolutional net (AlexNet) roughly halved the error rate of
    the ImageNet vision contest; the field reorganised within two years. The same recipe — layers,
    backprop, scale — now generates text, translates languages and folds proteins; the
    <strong>transformer</strong> architecture behind modern language models (2017) is the newest verse:
    an attention mechanism that lets every word in a sequence weigh every other, trained by exactly the
    guess-measure-step loop you computed above, at a scale where the parameter count of the widget's
    single w becomes a hundred billion.
  </p>

  <h2>A model you can hold in your head: training by hand</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing demystifies the loop like computing one step yourself. Take the simplest model, a line:
    prediction ŷ = w·x, one parameter w. Training data: two points, (1, 2) and (2, 4) — the true
    relationship is y = 2x. The <strong>loss function</strong> is mean squared error:
    L(w) = ½[(w·1 − 2)² + (w·2 − 4)²]. Start at w = 0: predictions are 0 and 0, errors are −2 and
    −4, loss is ½(4 + 16) = 10 — badly wrong, exactly like the first red dot in the plot. Now the
    gradient: dL/dw = (w − 2)·1 + (2w − 4)·2 = 5w − 10. At w = 0 the slope is −10 (steeply downhill
    to the right, as the picture shows). Take one step with learning rate η = 0.1: w ← 0 − 0.1×(−10)
    = 1.0. Recompute: predictions 1 and 2, loss ½(1 + 4) = 2.5. Second step: slope = 5×1 − 10 = −5,
    so w ← 1 + 0.5 = 1.5, loss = ½(0.25 + 1) = 0.625. Each step multiplies the error by (1 − 0.5)
    — the loss decays geometrically, w glides 0 → 1 → 1.5 → 1.75 → … toward 2, and the steps shrink
    because the slope shrinks. That arithmetic <em>is</em> the widget, in one dimension. Everything
    deep about the field is this loop repeated: in a neural network there are millions of w's, the
    gradient is computed by backpropagation rather than one derivative, an <strong>epoch</strong> is
    one full pass through the training data, a <strong>batch</strong> is the handful of examples each
    gradient is computed from (stochastic gradient descent), and the loss surface is no longer a
    friendly bowl — but "guess, measure, step" is unchanged, and you have now done it once yourself.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The gradient-descent picture hides real subtleties worth knowing. <strong>Loss surfaces are not
    bowls.</strong> The widget's valley has one bottom; real loss surfaces in high dimensions have
    saddle points, plateaus and many minima — remarkably, in deep networks most local minima are nearly
    as good as the global one, so "getting stuck" is less of a problem than feared. <strong>The
    learning rate is the master dial</strong>: too large and the steps leap across the valley and
    diverge; too small and training crawls or freezes on a plateau; practical training uses schedules
    or adaptive methods (Adam, 2014) that tune the effective step per parameter. <strong>Stochastic
    gradient descent</strong> computes the gradient on small random batches rather than the whole
    dataset — noisier steps, but thousands of times cheaper per step, and the noise itself seems to
    help generalisation. <strong>Beyond supervised:</strong> the models behind modern chat assistants
    are trained in stages — self-supervised pretraining (predict the next word in trillions of tokens,
    no labels needed), then fine-tuning with human feedback, where the loss is a human preference
    score. And the honest caveats: models inherit their datasets' biases (a hiring model learns the
    past's discrimination as signal), can be brittle off-distribution (a panda plus a carefully chosen
    sticker becomes, to the network, a gibbon with high confidence), and cannot tell you what they
    don't know. The discipline for all of it is unchanged from this lesson: every claim about an AI
    system should trace back to a loss function, a dataset and an evaluation on unseen data. That
    discipline — not the hype — is the field.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rumelhart, Hinton & Williams, "Learning representations by back-propagating errors", Nature (1986); LeCun et al. on convolutional networks (1989–98); Krizhevsky, Sutskever & Hinton (2012); Goodfellow, Bengio & Courville, Deep Learning (2016); Russell & Norvig, Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, chs. 19 & 21.</p>
